{"id":"sin","name":"Sin","aliases":[],"sources":["nave","torrey","easton"],"topics":["nave:sin","nave:sin:2","torrey:sin"],"groups":["Miscellany Of Minor Sub-Topics","Instances of unpardonable","Instances Of","1. Desert of, a wilderness between Elim and Sinai"],"definition":{"source":"easton","term":"Sin","text":"Is “any want of conformity unto or transgression of the law of God” (1 John 3:4; Rom. 4:15), in the inward state and habit of the soul, as well as in the outward conduct of the life, whether by omission or commission (Rom. 6:12-17; 7:5-24). It is “not a mere violation of the law of our constitution, nor of the system of things, but an offence against a personal lawgiver and moral governor who vindicates his law with penalties. The soul that sins is always conscious that his sin is (1) intrinsically vile and polluting, and (2) that it justly deserves punishment, and calls down the righteous wrath of God. Hence sin carries with it two inalienable characters, (1) ill-desert, guilt (reatus); and (2) pollution (macula).”, Hodge’s Outlines. The moral character of a man’s actions is determined by the moral state of his heart. The disposition to sin, or the habit of the soul that leads to the …"},"lemmas":[{"strong":"G266","lemma":"ἁμαρτία","xlit":"hamartía","gloss":"a sin","kjv":"offence, sin","count":80,"share":0.08},{"strong":"H2403","lemma":"חַטָּאָה","xlit":"chaṭṭâʼâh","gloss":"an offence , and its penalty, occasion, sacrifice, or ex","kjv":"punishment, purifying, sin","count":80,"share":0.08},{"strong":"H5771","lemma":"עָוֺן","xlit":"ʻâvôn","gloss":"perversity","kjv":"fault, iniquity, mischeif, punishment","count":74,"share":0.07},{"strong":"H2398","lemma":"חָטָא","xlit":"châṭâʼ","gloss":"to miss","kjv":"bear the blame, cleanse, commit, by fault","count":57,"share":0.05},{"strong":"H7563","lemma":"רָשָׁע","xlit":"râshâʻ","gloss":"morally wrong","kjv":"condemned, guilty, ungodly, wicked","count":44,"share":0.04},{"strong":"H6588","lemma":"פֶּשַׁע","xlit":"pesh